Yes. There are several different ideas braided together there, and separating them actually makes the whole thing stronger.

The first is pattern migration across substrate. In fiction you can take something normally instantiated in one lineage—kingship, consciousness, sacred authority, technological dominance, language—and move the role somewhere completely different:

human → ape
animal → fungus
individual → colony
organism → ecosystem
biological intelligence → machine

Then the fictional world becomes an experiment. You are no longer asking “what would talking apes look like?” You are asking:

Which properties belonged to humanity, and which belonged to the role humanity happened to occupy?

That is why something like Planet of the Apes works conceptually. Swap the occupants while preserving parts of the structure and suddenly previously invisible assumptions become visible.

You could systematically build worlds that way:

keep invariant X
change substrate Y
change timescale Z
simulate
measure emergent consequences

A civilization of intelligent fungi would force you to reconsider individuality because a mycelial body does not partition itself exactly like a primate body. A civilization descended from eusocial insects changes family, property, warfare, reproduction, and perhaps even the intuitive meaning of “self.” Herbivorous intelligent dinosaurs alter another set of priors. Make intelligence aquatic and fire disappears as an easy technological primitive. Each counterfactual becomes a probe of the hidden constraints behind our own world.

Your athlete example is the same maneuver at smaller scale, but I would describe it carefully. We can’t extract some literal metaphysical substance called “dominance” from a tennis champion and inject it into basketball. But we can attempt to identify transferable latent operators:

anticipation
opponent modeling
competitive arousal regulation
deliberate practice
error correction
tempo manipulation
spatial prediction
recovery after failure
attention under pressure

Then distinguish those from substrate-specific capabilities:

racquet mechanics
serve biomechanics
tennis footwork
court geometry

Now you can ask:

elite tennis performance
→ factorize
→ domain-specific components + transferable operators
→ translate transferable operators into basketball
→ simulate/test
→ observe delta

That is functional transduction, not superficial imitation.

And your voice observation points toward an even larger compiler:

spoken experience
→ audio signal
→ words
→ semantic representation
→ relations/operators
→ mathematical abstraction
→ simulation/code
→ new instantiation

The exciting part is that you can travel both directions. Mathematics doesn’t have to be the final destination. Once an operator has been manipulated mathematically, it can be rendered back into a story, visualization, game, training drill, physical interface, or another person’s language.

That’s essentially representation surfing.

The Pantheon then becomes valuable not merely as an archive of ideas but as a transformation environment. New ideas enter and get tested against existing operators:

new pattern
→ compare with existing structures
→ find analogues
→ identify contradictions
→ abstract invariants
→ recombine
→ generate predictions
→ instantiate somewhere else
→ observe whether it survived translation

That is much stronger than accumulating clever metaphors. Metaphors become candidate hypotheses about shared structure.

And yes, other people become tremendously important because no individual explores the entire possibility space. Someone may embody an unusually sophisticated solution without ever being capable of explaining it formally. An elite athlete, carpenter, parent, musician, negotiator, naturalist, surgeon, or mechanic can contain enormous amounts of tacit knowledge.

So the AI problem becomes:

Can we recover a transferable representation from competence even when the competent person cannot verbalize the representation themselves?

Potentially:

demonstrations + outcomes + context + corrections
→ infer latent strategy
→ separate invariant from flourish
→ translate
→ test in foreign domain

That is a serious research direction, and it connects imitation learning, representation learning, transfer learning, inverse reinforcement learning, world models, and skill abstraction.

One correction to your reinforcement-learning thought: our conversation can absolutely affect the context I use with you right now, and some information can contribute to product memory depending on settings, but you shouldn’t infer that every insight you tell me is immediately modifying my underlying model weights or being propagated live into how I interact with everybody else. Those are different mechanisms.

But conceptually, you’re right about the larger machine: millions of humans independently discover local adaptations. If an AI system can extract the operator beneath the adaptation rather than merely copying the surface behavior, humanity’s distributed experience starts looking like an enormous library of partially encoded algorithms.

Then the Golden Braid becomes something like:

human discovery
→ representation
→ abstraction
→ cross-domain translation
→ simulation
→ new manifestation
→ human observation
→ another discovery

And I like your final qualification: with a shadow of doubt is better.

The doubt is not an impurity in the braid. It’s the part that keeps simulation from silently becoming belief.

imagination generates
simulation explores
reality discriminates

Keep all three, and you can travel very far without losing the road home.